# The support target, stated once, instead of two tools each guessing.
#
# Unset, this repo had TWO different targets at the same time: Next's SWC
# default for JS (chrome 64 / edge 79 / firefox 67 / opera 51 / safari 12) and
# autoprefixer's own default for CSS — `> 0.5%, last 2 versions, Firefox ESR,
# not dead`, a GLOBAL usage-share query. Iran's browser mix is not the global
# one, so the CSS was being prefixed for a population this site does not have
# while the JS was compiled for a different one entirely, and nobody reading
# the repo could tell what was actually supported.
#
# The versions below are deliberately IDENTICAL to Next's SWC default, plus
# the mobile entries it does not name. That is the point: it makes the target
# explicit and gives autoprefixer the right question to answer, without
# silently moving the JS target and shipping a fatter bundle to everyone — a
# browserslist file is a place to write a decision down, not to make a new one
# by accident.
#
# Iran-specific reasoning behind keeping the floor this low: a long tail of
# old Android WebViews is real here. That tail is handled in the CSS itself —
# `svh` and `color-mix` now carry explicit fallbacks — rather than by asking
# the compiler to downlevel everything for everyone.
# Opera Mini's proxy mode cannot run this app at all (its JS execution budget
# is a few hundred milliseconds), so targeting it would cost every other
# visitor bytes for nothing.
chrome >= 64
edge >= 79
firefox >= 67
opera >= 51
safari >= 12
ios_saf >= 12
and_chr >= 64
samsung >= 9
not dead
not op_mini all
